1	//! Block quote parsing for Markdown (CommonMark §5.1).
2	//!
3	//! A block quote begins with `>` at the start of a line and can contain
4	//! nested block elements. Multiple consecutive `>` lines form a single quote.
5	//! Nested quotes are created with `>>`, `>>>`, etc.
6	//!
7	//! # CommonMark §5.1 Block Quotes
8	//!
9	//! A block quote marker consists of 0-3 spaces of indentation, `>`, and an
10	//! optional space. The contents of the block quote are the result of parsing
11	//! the remainder of the line (after the `>` and optional space) as blocks.
12	//!
13	//! ## Depth Limits
14	//!
15	//! To prevent stack overflow from pathological input (e.g., hundreds of `>`),
16	//! nesting depth is limited by `MarkdownParserOptions::max_nesting_depth`
17	//! (default: 100). Deeper nesting emits a diagnostic and treats additional
18	//! `>` as content.
19	//!
20	//! ## Lazy Continuation (§5.1)
21	//!
22	//! A block quote can contain "lazy continuation lines" — paragraph content
23	//! that continues without requiring `>` on each line. For example:
24	//!
25	//! ```markdown
26	//! > This is a quote
27	//! that continues here without >
28	//! ```
29	//!
30	//! Both lines belong to the same block quote. Lazy continuation stops at:
31	//! - A blank line
32	//! - A line that starts another block-level construct (header, code, list, etc.)
33	
